Description: hw/arm/chumby_classic: Add a pen-down control, and document the handoff's interrupts
 With pen-down=on the pen is held down from just after each reset, so a test
 can hold it through a boot without racing it. With handoff=on, that is a
 falling edge on PE19, which stock leaves unmasked, on the interrupt line all
 six GPIO ports share: it stays asserted until port E's status is cleared.
Author: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [AI generated code]
Signed-off-by: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B1fN5HecVTggWQg4Zt8Srt
---
diff --git a/docs/system/arm/chumby-classic.rst b/docs/system/arm/chumby-classic.rst
--- a/docs/system/arm/chumby-classic.rst
+++ b/docs/system/arm/chumby-classic.rst
@@ -109,6 +109,10 @@ slow by that much, as on a real one.
 ``-M chumby-classic,handoff=on`` starts the machine as a kernel started
 from the running stock firmware does: the LCD's graphic window on and
 opaque, the pins of ports B, D and E as stock set them, and noise in RAM.
+That includes stock's interrupt state: PE19 unmasked (the pen), with other
+interrupts pending but masked. All six GPIO ports share one interrupt line,
+level-style, so with ``pen-down=on`` a pen held through the start asserts it
+until something clears port E's status, however the kernel handles it.
 
 USB devices attach to the host controller's root hub, for example a
 disk image as a USB stick and a keyboard::
@@ -248,6 +252,8 @@ the identity can also be read and set at any time with ``qom-get`` and
 - ``internal-hub`` (bool): the board's USB hub on root hub port 2, behind
   which devices given no port attach; on by default; fixed once the
   machine is created
+- ``pen-down`` (bool): the pen held down on the touchscreen, whatever the
+  pointer does; given at the start, from just after each reset
 - ``handoff`` (bool): start as a kernel started from the running stock
   firmware does; off by default; fixed once the machine is created
 
